      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-223638",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the following statement:\n\n> Let $a$ and $b$ be integers and $n$ be a positive integer. If $gcd(a, n) = 1$, then the linear congruence $ax\u2261b(modn)$ has a solution.\n\nA proof of this statement is given below.\n\n> $L1$: We are given that $gcd(a, n) = 1$.\n> $L2$: Then, $\u2203u, v \u2208 Z, au + nv = 1$.\n> $L3$: This means that $au + nv\u22611(modn)$.\n> $L4$: So, we obtain $au\u22611(modn)$.\n> $L5$: And, in turn, $aub\u2261b(modn)$.\n> $L6$: Therefore, a solution to the congruence is $x\u2261ub(modn)$.\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\nLine $L2$ follows from line $L1$ by {{field-1}}.\n\nLine $L5$ follows from line {{field-2}} by {{field-3}}.\n\nLine $L6$ follows from line $L5$ by {{field-4}}"
    }
